  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A widespread, soaking rainfall will arrive later\nThanksgiving Day, with unsettled weather lasting into the weekend.\n\nStrong high pressure over the Northeast will drift into New England \ntoday, but continue to maintain dry weather through tonight. \n\nNorthern and southern stream energy will phase with a large upper \nlevel low taking shape over the Tennessee Valley, as broad surface \nlow pressure tracks north into the Carolinas tonight with a trough \nextending westward into the lower Ohio Valley. The system is then \nforecast to continue northward into the eastern Great Lakes and \nNortheast later Thursday into Friday. Confidence remains high that \nwidespread precipitation will spread northward into the region on \nThursday as plume of deep moisture transport develops within an area \nof strong deformation along the north flank of the upper low. \n\nDry conditions should begin Thanksgiving day as high pressure \nmaintains some influence, while continuing to slide to the east.\nRain should get into the southern Tier sometime after \nmidday/early afternoon, then slowly spread northward through the\nremainder of the day into early evening. The North Country may \neven remain rain free through daylight hours. Strong easterly \njet aloft and tight surface pressure gradient will also yield \nbreezy to windy conditions Thursday afternoon through Thursday \nnight, though winds are expected to remain below advisory \ncriteria, but can't rule out peak gusts of 35 to 40 mph.\n\nTemperatures aloft remain quite mild with profiles suggesting almost \nall rain as the primary precipitation type through Friday morning. \nConfidence remains very low for any wintry precipitation with \ntemperatures aloft well above freezing during this time, very low \ndew points at the onset of precipitation may allow wet-bulbing \neffect to very briefly bring temperatures down near the freezing \nmark, bringing the potential for a small window of sleet or freezing \nrain in pockets across the higher terrain areas before quickly \nswitching over to plain rain. \n\nFriday through Saturday, occasional showers will continue as the \ndeep mid level closed low crosses the region. The airmass will grow \ncold enough for some lake enhancement by Friday and Saturday, with\nindications pointing towards westerly flow during this time frame,\ngradually becoming southwesterly late Saturday into Saturday \nnight. Late Friday through Saturday, modest cold advection will \ndrop 850 MB temperatures down to -2C to -3C. Latest sounding \ndata and thickness levels suggest rain for lower elevations, but\nsome wet snow may start to mix in across higher terrain. Marginal\ntemperatures at the surface and aloft suggest there will be \nlittle, if any snow accumulation during this period, even across\nhigher terrain. Best chance for a light coating would be across\nthe higher terrain of the western Southern Tier.\n\nRainfall totals with this system targeting the 1.0\" to 1.5\" range, \nwhile acknowledging the 75th percentile of ensemble guidance does \napproach 2\" across western New York. With much of the area in D1 \nmoderate drought after the exceptionally dry conditions since \nSeptember, this is expected to be a beneficial rainfall with minimal \nhydro concerns at this time.",
